#---
# purpose: "one lambda driven by Enum.each both through a grounded call and through the boxed seam settles on one return convention"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.155's two-sided witness. `double` is reached BOTH ways in one program:
# the first `Enum.each` names it, so its each-step callsite calls the lambda's
# own executable directly; the mailbox round trip erases that name, so the
# second `Enum.each` reaches the very same lambda through its construction
# wrapper.
#
# The wrapper's public return form is derived from its MEMBERS -- the lambda's
# own return -- and only the grounded callsite reaches that return. So the two
# callsites cannot be allowed to answer "how many lanes come back?"
# independently: when the grounded callsite's discarded result narrowed the
# member to zero lanes while the boxed callsite expected the wrapper's one, the
# wrapper wrote its returned value into the register the continuation reads as
# its own closure pointer and the run aborted in `fz_closure_get_capture_atom`
# after the FIRST boxed element, on JIT and AOT alike.
#
# `:done` proves control came back past both halves.
fn main() do
  double = fn (x) -> dbg(x * 2) end
  Enum.each([1, 2], double)

  send(self(), double)
  boxed = receive do f -> f end
  Enum.each([3, 4], boxed)
  dbg(:done)
end
